      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Certainly, the opposition to the labor movement was strong and ideological, not solely focused on \"greedy\" corporations. Conservative and big capital interests often argued that strong unions would stifle free-market competition and that high wages would make the nation uncompetitive globally.\n\nThis perspective, often supported by political elites, framed the organized worker not as a necessary part of the system, but as a radical force demanding government overreach and fundamentally undermining the \"right to contract\" in the free market.\n\nThese arguments typically positioned the conflict as a battle between individual liberty (unfettered by regulation) and the collective rights of the working class.",
