      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You are accurately pointing out the ideological divide in the debate, as \"free market\" arguments often served to defend the pre-existing power structure. When absolute power rested with the employer, the \"freedom to contract\" often meant the freedom to accept deeply unfair terms, negating the concept of true liberty for the worker.\n\nFrom a progressive standpoint, the rise of strong union power and labor law is seen as necessary regulation to correct natural power imbalances, effectively defining a **social right to a fair life** alongside the economic right to work.\n\nThis perspective views the 20th-century gains not as a deviation from freedom, but as the *establishment of the necessary conditions for meaningful liberty for the majority of the population to thrive and participate politically in a strong democracy.",
